%N A099650 Solutions to x+phi(x) = sigma(x)/2.
%C A099650 If 5*2^n-1 is prime then m=3*2^(n+1)*(5*2^n-1) is in the sequence because m+phi(m)=2^(n+1)*3*(5*2^n-1)+2^(n+1)*(5*2^n-2)=2^(n+1) *(20*2^n-5)=2^(n+1)*5*(2^(n+2)-1)=1/2*4*(2^(n+2)-1)*(5*2^n)= 1/2*sigma(3)*sigma(2^(n+1))*sigma(5*2^n-1)=1/2*sigma(3*2^(n+1) *(5*2^n-1))=1/2*sigma(m). So 3*2^(A001770+1)*(5*2^A001770-1) is a subsequence of this sequence. - _Farideh Firoozbakht_, Aug 04 2005; edited by _Max Alekseyev_, Oct 18 2026
%H A099650 Max Alekseyev, <a href="/A099650/b099650.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..71</a>
%e A099650 n=456: phi(456) = 144, sigma(456) = 1200.
%t A099650 Do[If[DivisorSigma[1, m] == 2m + 2 EulerPhi[m], Print[m]], {m, 100000000}] (Firoozbakht)
%Y A099650 Cf. A000010, A000203, A001770.
%Y A099650 Solutions to sigma(m) = 2*m + k*phi(m): A008578 (k=-1), A000396 (k=0), A088826 (k=1), A099650 (k=2), A392691 (k=3), A392692 (k=4), A392693 (k=5).
%K A099650 nonn,changed
%O A099650 1,1
